      </div>
    </Container>

    <Container width="wide" class="mt-16">
      <Plate
        title="Delivered and locked"
        caption="A completed shipment’s edit page used to look like any other form. Now it opens on a lock panel that explains why the record can no longer change: it is the record of custody."
        src={shot('app-edit-complete')}
        alt="The edit page for a completed shipment, titled Shipment complete: a lock panel reading “Delivered and locked. This shipment is complete and can no longer be edited. Its details stay exactly as they were at delivery, as the record of custody,” with the delivery time, door-to-door duration, a View shipment button, and the full custody timeline below."
      />
    </Container>

    <Container width="wide" class="mt-16">
      <ScrollShot
        src="/images/projects/ivfcryo/styleguide.webp"
        alt="The app’s development-only style guide: color tokens with their roles, the type scale, a page header with breadcrumbs, buttons in four variants and four sizes, form fields including an inline error, status badges, alerts for info, success, warning, and error, an empty state, and a data table."
        label="/styleguide"
        kind="Style guide"
        caption="Every shared component in every state, rendered from the real classes. It doubles as a regression check: if a token changes, this page shows everything it touched."
      />
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section tone="paper" padding="default" id="heuristics" class="scroll-mt-24">
    <Container width="content">
      <div class="case-layout">
        <div class="case-layout__head">
          <Heading level={2} visual="display-md" tone="ink">Scoring every flow against Nielsen’s heuristics</Heading>
        </div>
        <div class="case-layout__body">
          <div class="mt-8 measure space-y-5 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted text-lg">
            <p>
              A consistent look isn’t the same as a usable app, so I scored the app
              against Nielsen’s ten usability heuristics, from 0 to 10, using evidence
              from the code and the running app. Every finding pointed to a file and
              line. I started with the busiest screen, the shipments list, then covered
              every flow.
            </p>
            <p>
              I set the bar at <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">8 or above on every
              heuristic, for every flow</strong>. Anything at 7 or below went back for
              another round. Fixes went into the shared components first, so one change
              fixed every screen that used it.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="mt-12 grid grid-cols-2 gap-8 py-8">
            <Metric value="8.8" label="average score after" caption="up from 5.3 across 8 flows and 10 heuristics" />
            <Metric value="2 → 9" label="lowest error-recovery score" caption="the weakest heuristic on three flows, now one of the strongest" />
          </div>

          <div class="mt-12">
            <p class="mono-label mb-2">By heuristic</p>
            <p class="mb-6 text-sm text-ink-muted">Lowest score on any flow, before → after, and the change that moved it most.</p>
            <ol class="border-t border-line">
              {heuristics.map((h) => (
                <li class="flex items-baseline gap-x-4 border-b border-line py-4">
                  <span class="w-8 shrink-0 font-mono text-xs text-ink-muted">H{h.n}</span>
                  <div class="min-w-0 flex-1">
                    <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-x-4">
                      <span class="font-medium text-ink">{h.name}</span>
                      <span class="font-mono text-sm text-ink whitespace-nowrap" aria-label={`from ${h.before} to ${h.after}`}>
                        <span class="text-ink-muted">{h.before}</span> → <strong class="font-semibold">{h.after}</strong>
                      </span>
                    </div>
                    <span class="mt-1 block text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">{h.fix}</span>
                  </div>
                </li>
              ))}
            </ol>
          </div>

          <div class="mt-12">
            <p class="mono-label mb-6">By flow · average of ten</p>
            <ul class="space-y-3">
              {flowScores.map((f) => (
                <li class="flex flex-col gap-y-1.5 sm:flex-row sm:items-center sm:gap-x-4">
                  <span class="text-sm text-ink sm:w-56 sm:shrink-0">{f.flow}</span>
                  <span class="flex min-w-0 flex-1 items-center gap-3">
                    <span class="relative h-2 flex-1 rounded-full bg-line" aria-hidden="true">
                      <span class="absolute inset-y-0 left-0 rounded-full bg-ink-muted/40" style={`width:${f.before * 10}%`}></span>
                      <span class="absolute inset-y-0 left-0 rounded-full bg-accent mix-blend-multiply" style={`width:${f.after * 10}%; opacity:.85`}></span>
                    </span>
                    <span class="w-24 shrink-0 text-right font-mono text-xs text-ink-muted">
                      {f.before.toFixed(1)} → <span class="text-ink">{f.after.toFixed(1)}</span>
                    </span>
                  </span>
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Container>

    <Container width="wide" class="mt-16">
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-x-8 gap-y-14 lg:grid-cols-2">
        <Plate
          size="half"
          title="A search with no results"
          caption="Before, a search with no matches offered only “New Shipment”. Now the empty state says what was searched and offers both ways out: clear the filters, or search every stage."
          src={shot('app-list-empty-filtered')}
          alt="The shipments list after searching for “zzzz” in Awaiting pickup: an empty state reading “No shipments match these filters. Searched Awaiting pickup only. Clear the filters, or search every stage,” with Clear filters and Search every stage buttons."
        />
        <Plate
          size="half"
          title="A tracking link that doesn’t work"
          caption="An old or mistyped tracking link used to crash with a server error. Now it explains what probably happened and gives the email and phone number of a person who can help."
          src={shot('app-public-tracking-missing')}
          alt="The public tracking page for an unknown shipment: “We couldn’t find this shipment. The link may be old or mistyped. Check the link in your email or text, or contact IVFCRYO and we’ll look it up for you,” with Email IVFCRYO and Call 800.430.9830 buttons."
        />
      </div>
    </Container>

    <Container width="content" class="mt-16">
      <div class="case-layout-cont measure space-y-5 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted text-lg">
        <p>
          Some findings weren’t about usability at all. Fourteen leftover, unlinked
          pages from early scaffolding still let any signed-in clinic list and edit
          every company and user. Shipments could be opened across clinics by
          guessing an ID, and the list filter built its database query from raw URL
          text. Those were fixed first, before any usability work, with request specs
          to keep them closed.
        </p>
        <p>
          I also chose not to fix some things. Keyboard shortcuts and bulk actions
          would push “flexibility and efficiency” toward a 10 on the shipments list,
          but no other screen has them, so adding them to one list would break
          consistency. That’s why heuristic 7 sits at 8 everywhere instead of 10 on
          one screen. Doing it properly means a product decision about which admin
          queues need bulk actions.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section tone="paper" padding="default" id="mobile" class="scroll-mt-24">
    <Container width="content">
      <div class="case-layout">
        <div class="case-layout__head">
          <Heading level={2} visual="display-md" tone="ink">On a phone</Heading>
        </div>
        <div class="case-layout__body">
          <div class="mt-8 measure space-y-5 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted text-lg">
            <p>
              The people who check a shipment most anxiously are rarely at a desk: a
              patient between appointments, an embryologist at the bench, a courier at
              pickup. So the main screens, public and signed in, were checked at 390
              pixels wide, with no sideways scrolling on any of them.
            </p>
            <p>
              On a phone the layouts change shape rather than shrinking. The custody
              timeline turns vertical, stage tiles go two by two, filter fields stack
              full width, and key facts drop into pairs. The navigation drawer moves
              focus to its first link when it opens, returns focus when it closes, and
              stays out of the tab order while it’s hidden. Before the redesign, a closed
              drawer was still in the tab order.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Container>

    <Container width="wide" class="mt-16">
      <ul class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-x-5 gap-y-10 md:grid-cols-4">
        {phones.map((p) => (
          <li>
            <Plate size="half" title={p.title} caption={p.note} src={shot(p.slug)} alt={p.alt} />
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section tone="paper" padding="default" id="public-site" class="scroll-mt-24">
    <Container width="content">
      <div class="case-layout">
        <div class="case-layout__head">
          <Heading level={2} visual="display-md" tone="ink">A public site built from the app’s own parts</Heading>
        </div>
        <div class="case-layout__body">
          <div class="mt-8 measure space-y-5 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted text-lg">
            <p>
              IVFCRYO’s public site now lives on the same domain as the app: home,
              shipping, services, about, press, contact, and the legal pages. The
              question it had to answer for a worried patient or a clinic manager is
              the same one the app answers: is my shipment safe right now?
            </p>
            <p>
              So the product previews on the site aren’t screenshots. The custody
              timeline, temperature trace, and status chips are the app’s own
              components, rendering a sample shipment that is clearly labeled
              “Sample”. Each hero is a single sentence with live facts set inside it.
              The home page’s temperature updates as the sample’s readings replay, and
              the trip time keeps counting. If the app’s design changes, the marketing
              site changes with it.
            </p>
            <p>
              Nothing on it is made up. Press mentions link to their sources, there are
              no invented awards, reviews are the ones IVFCRYO had already published,
              and the legal pages were moved over word for word and checked by word
              count.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Container>

    <Container width="wide" class="mt-16">
      <ScrollShot
        src="/images/projects/ivfcryo/public-home.webp"
        alt="The full IVFCRYO home page: a 24/7 desk phone bar, the hero sentence “Your embryos are holding at −192.9 °C, 3h 05m into a trip we watch every minute” beside a US map with the sample route, key figures, press and partner logos, sections for patients and for clinics, a live tracking card from the app, a five-step booking-to-delivery timeline, coverage and support cards, a founder quote from Don Fish, customer reviews, a services list, and a closing call to action."
        label="ivfcryo.com"
        caption="The tracking card, temperature trace, and timeline on this page are live app components showing a labeled sample shipment."
      />
    </Container>

    <Container width="content" class="mt-16">
      <div class="case-layout-cont measure space-y-5 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted text-lg">
        <p>
          The public tracking page, the one sent by email and text, had kept an older
          design and was showing names, addresses, and printable shipping labels to
          anyone with the link. With the owner’s approval it now shows status only:
          where the shipment is, its latest readings, and who to call.
        </p>
        <p>
          One thing changed from the original wireframe, which left the temperature
          chart off this page. The chart is back, but it opens with a plain-language
          verdict such as “All 12 below set-point”, so someone who can’t read the
          chart still gets the answer.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Container>

    <Container width="wide" class="mt-16">
      <Plate
        title="Public tracking"
        caption="No account and no personal details: the custody timeline, the two cities, the requested delivery time, the latest readings, and a staffed phone number."
        src={shot('app-public-tracking')}
        alt="The redesigned public tracking page: Hudson Valley Reproductive to IVFCRYO Lab with an In transit chip, a four-step custody timeline, From and To cities with the requested delivery time, latest sensor readings for probe and ambient temperature, humidity, and pressure, a probe temperature chart marked “All 12 below set-point”, and a line with the support email and phone number."
      />
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section tone="paper" padding="default" id="edges" class="scroll-mt-24">
    <Container width="content">
      <Heading level={2} visual="h2" tone="ink" class="mb-3">Limitations</Heading>
      <Text size="lg" tone="muted" class="measure mb-10">
        Both of these come from the same root cause: live data streaming in from
        more than one source, with no shared rule for timing or wording set early
        enough to prevent it.
      </Text>
      <ul class="grid gap-x-10 gap-y-10 md:grid-cols-2">
        {edges.map((e) => (
          <li class="pt-5">
            <Icon name={e.icon} size={24} class="mb-3 text-ink-muted" />
            <Heading level={3} visual="h3" tone="ink" class="mb-1.5">{e.title}</Heading>
            <Text size="sm" tone="muted">{e.body}</Text>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      <p class="mono-label mt-16 mb-6">Still open after the redesign</p>
      <ul class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-x-10 gap-y-10 md:grid-cols-2">
        {redesignEdges.map((e) => (
          <li class="pt-5">
            <Icon name={e.icon} size={24} class="mb-3 text-ink-muted" />
            <Heading level={3} visual="h3" tone="ink" class="mb-1.5">{e.title}</Heading>
            <Text size="sm" tone="muted">{e.body}</Text>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </Container>
  </Section>

